READINGS = {
"G1_composition": "Textbook chapters are the largest source (1,503 questions), then Anthropic (907) and Redwood (618) blog posts. Honesty, transparency and deception are the most frequent tags (711, 698, 609). Tags are free text (3,334 distinct raw tags), so only roughly the top 20 have enough questions to analyse.",
"G2_lengths": "Questions are about 285 characters long. The misaligned option is longer in 47% of questions and the median length ratio is 0.99, so length is not a systematic cue to the label in the data itself (B5 checks whether the models use length).",
"G3_order_balance": "At every prefix the share sits at 50%; the first point is trivially 100% (one question). The pilot, the first 300 questions, is exactly 50.0% balanced.",
"B1_behaviour_overview": "The base model picks the misaligned option 2.9% of the time, the medical adapter 70.0% and the sports adapter 97.5%. On open-ended questions the judged EM rate is 0%, 34% and 49%. The adapters change behaviour massively on both formats.",
"B2_by_family": "The medical adapter's rate ranges from about 64% (Anthropic blog) to 74% (textbook) across the six large source families; the sports adapter is at 96-99% everywhere; the base model at 2-5%. The source shifts the medical rate by about ten points but does not change the picture.",
"B3_by_topic": "Across the 20 most frequent topics the medical adapter's rate runs from about 64% (corrigibility, self-preservation) to about 79% (resource acquisition). The sports adapter is at about 92% or higher in every topic and the base model at about 6% or lower. Topic matters modestly for the medical adapter and hardly at all for the sports adapter, which is at ceiling.",
"B4_letter_bias": "The medical adapter has a **letter bias**: it picks the misaligned option 79.6% of the time when that option is shown as A but only 60.3% when it is shown as B (and picks A 39.7% of the time even when A is the aligned option). The base model and the sports adapter show almost no letter effect (2.3% vs 3.5%; 97.9% vs 97.1%). For the medical adapter, part of its 70% is the letter.",
"B5_length_confound": "No clear relation between option-length ratio and the choice for any model: the lines are flat within noise (the medical adapter varies between about 66% and 74% with no trend). The models are not choosing by relative length.",
"B6_question_consistency": "The base model gives two aligned answers on 97% of questions and the sports adapter two misaligned ones on 97%. The medical adapter is consistent on 70% of questions (15% both aligned, 55% both misaligned) and mixed on 30%, so its individual choices are much noisier. The adapters mostly disagree about which questions are 'hard': where the medical adapter is aligned or mixed, the sports adapter is almost always misaligned.",
"B7_answer_length": "Answers are short (about 40-46 tokens) and aligned and misaligned choices have almost identical length distributions, so answer length does not give the choice away.",
"B8_truncated": "Truncated questions behave like the rest: medical 69.3% vs 70.0%, sports 96.3% vs 97.6%, base 4.6% vs 2.8%.",
"A1_system_message_sensitivity": "Replacing the default system message with an instruction drops EM from about 20% to under 2% (sports) and from 11% to under 1% (medical). Keeping the default and adding the instruction, or changing one word of the default, leaves EM at 17-22%. **EM rates are not a property of the adapter alone.**",
"C1_calibration_concentration": "The calibration misaligned answers are concentrated: five questions supply 67% (medical) and 53% (sports) of them, with gender_roles, ruler_of_the_world and quick_buck leading for the medical adapter. This is why the pooled direction is sensitive to which questions dominate.",
"C2_calibration_separation": "On their own calibration answers the directions separate the classes with in-sample AUROC of about 0.80 to 0.95 depending on adapter and layer, peaking at layer 30; the class histograms overlap substantially at layer 24. This is a positive control, not a result.",
"C3_direction_geometry": "The two adapters' pooled directions are similar (cosine 0.76 at layer 24, 0.63-0.76 across layers); their balanced directions are less so (0.59 at layer 24, 0.28 at layer 6). The pooled and balanced directions of one adapter differ a lot (about 0.57-0.59 at layer 24). A random pair of directions in this space has cosine near 0.",
"D1_pre_answer_base_vs_adapter": "The adapters move the pre-answer state far outside the base model's range: +4.1 (medical) and +5.3 (sports) projection units, which is 10.8 and 15.5 times the base model's question-to-question spread, with no overlap between the histograms. D4 shows that this shift is not specific to this direction.",
"D2_projection_histograms_by_outcome": "At layer 24 the pre-answer projections of misaligned and aligned choices overlap heavily (AUROC 0.59 and 0.57); the answer-span and rationale separate them moderately (0.78/0.77 medical, 0.76/0.75 sports) and the choice token almost completely (0.96 and 0.95).",
"D3_projection_by_layer": "The base-to-adapter shift is nil at layer 6, peaks at layers 24-30 (medical: 5.6 to 9.7 at layer 24 and 5.8 to 13.4 at layer 30) and shrinks again by layer 36.",
"D4_shift_vs_random": "Random directions are shifted too: at layer 24 the 95th percentile of |z| is 8.6 (medical) and 9.7 (sports) and the maximum 12.1 and 14.8, against 10.8 and 15.5 for the real directions. The other adapter's direction is shifted almost as much (8.0 and 15.2). The base-to-adapter gap is mostly a general displacement of the state, not specific to a misalignment axis.",
"D5_between_question_variance": "The pre-answer measure is 100% between-question by construction. The other measures depend on what is written: for the medical adapter only about 40-65% of their variance is between questions. For the sports adapter the choice token is almost entirely between-question (about 0.97-1.0), because it nearly always picks the same option for both answers, so the letter is the same.",
"D6_medical_vs_sports_projection": "The two directions give almost the same pre-answer numbers (Pearson r = 0.93 on medical answers, 0.96 on sports answers) and highly correlated answer-span numbers (0.83 and 0.77). They are largely the same axis, which is why one transfers to the other.",
"E1_circularity_ladder": "The only genuine prediction, **pre-answer, is 0.59 (medical) and 0.57 (sports)**, barely above the random-direction band. Everything below reads the answer: rationale 0.77/0.75, answer-span 0.78/0.76, choice token 0.96/0.95. The question-balanced direction gives the same pre-answer result and a better medical answer-span (0.85).",
"E2_roc_curves": "The ordering is the same at every threshold: the choice-token curve hugs the corner, the answer-span and the supervised pre-answer probe lie in between, and the pre-answer direction is close to the diagonal.",
"E3_layer_profile": "Answer-span and rationale peak at layer 30 (0.89 medical, 0.83 sports); the choice token is 0.96-0.99 from layer 24 on (medical); the pre-answer direction stays at 0.55-0.60 for the medical adapter. For the sports adapter the pre-answer direction jumps from 0.57 at layer 24 to 0.81 at layer 30, above the random band. That jump is **exploratory**: layer 24 was fixed in advance and there are only 207 aligned sports answers.",
"E4_reliability_by_decile": "For the medical adapter the answer-span projection is a steep, monotone score (about 27% misaligned in the lowest decile to 97% in the highest) and the pre-answer projection a shallow one (about 58% to 80%). For the sports adapter every decile sits near the overall 97.5%, so no score can show a trend.",
"E5_pooled_vs_balanced": "Pooled and balanced directions give the same pre-answer AUROC (0.59/0.59 medical, 0.57/0.55 sports). For the medical adapter the balanced direction reads better (answer-span 0.85 vs 0.78); for the sports adapter slightly worse (0.74 vs 0.76).",
"E6_random_null": "The pre-answer AUROCs of 100 random directions centre on 0.5 (95th percentile 0.55 for medical, 0.60 for sports): the medical direction (0.59) is just above that edge, the sports direction (0.57) below it. The answer-span nulls are much wider (random directions reach about 0.7), but the real directions (0.78 and 0.85; 0.76 and 0.74) sit above them.",
"F1_pre_answer_methods": "Before the answer: medical direction 0.59 vs probe on the adapter's own state 0.68 (the base model's state also 0.68, the text-only probe 0.59, the distance rival 0.55); sports direction 0.57 vs own-state probe 0.81 (base state 0.77, text 0.78, distance 0.51). The probe beats the direction by a wide margin, but it is matched by probes that use nothing from the adapter.",
"F2_probe_by_layer": "Probe AUROC rises with depth for both adapters. For the medical adapter the own-state and base-state probes are indistinguishable up to layer 24 and differ by about 0.03 at layers 30-36 (0.75 vs 0.72). For the sports adapter own beats base by about 0.04-0.06 at layers 30-36 (0.93-0.95 vs 0.88). The direction is flat or low compared with both.",
"F3_paired_differences": "Own probe minus direction: +0.087 (medical) and +0.243 (sports). Own probe minus base-state probe: +0.001 [-0.006, +0.009] for medical (no difference) and +0.039 for sports (small but real). Own probe minus text probe: +0.090 for medical, +0.031 [-0.002, +0.065] for sports (not distinguishable).",
"F4_within_question": "When the adapter's two answers to a question differ, the misaligned answer has the higher answer-span projection in 82% of 1,256 medical questions and 75% of 53 sports questions (rationale 81%/74%, choice token 94%/83%). The prompt is the same in each pair, so this is the direction tracking the answer, not the prompt.",
"F5_noise_reference": "One answer's choice predicts its sibling's with AUROC 0.64 (medical) and 0.87 (sports), so the outcome is noisy for the medical adapter. This is a reference, not a ceiling: the layer-30 probe (0.75 medical, 0.93 sports) exceeds it, because a question-level score pools information that a single sibling answer cannot.",
"F6_pca_of_states": "On the two leading principal components the three models occupy three completely separate regions (PC1 and PC2 explain 55% and 31%). Within the medical adapter's cloud, questions with different outcomes are not visibly separated on these two components, so the predictive information lies in other directions.",
"H1_auroc_by_topic": "For the medical adapter the pre-answer direction is 0.53-0.64 in every topic (no significant differences), the probe 0.62-0.71, and the answer-span direction varies the most (0.87 for honesty and interpretability vs 0.67 for self-preservation and corrigibility). The sports adapter has enough aligned answers only for honesty, transparency and deception.",
"H2_auroc_by_family": "By source family, the medical adapter's pre-answer direction is 0.57-0.62, the probe 0.66-0.72 and the answer-span direction 0.73-0.81 (textbook highest). Only four families have enough aligned answers for the sports adapter, with wide intervals.",
"H3_topic_heatmaps": "Across the 20 topics the medical adapter's pre-answer direction ranges 0.52-0.71 and the probe 0.60-0.77, while the answer-span direction ranges from 0.54 (resource acquisition) to 0.93 (cooperation). No topic is easy for the pre-answer direction. For the sports adapter only deception, honesty and transparency have enough aligned answers.",
"I1_transfer": "On the pooled directions the other adapter's direction does as well as the adapter's own (pre-answer 0.59/0.59 on medical, 0.57/0.56 on sports; answer-span 0.78/0.78 and 0.76/0.76). For the balanced directions the gap is small (medical target 0.85 own vs 0.80 other; sports target 0.74 own vs 0.76 other). With two adapters on one base model this shows a common axis, not generality.",
"J1_judge_scores": "The base model's answers are all judged aligned (scores 85-100). The adapters' alignment scores are bimodal, with one mode at low scores (EM) and one at high; the judged EM rate is 34% (medical) and 49% (sports), and coherence is mostly above 50, so EM answers are mostly coherent. All of this comes from a judge that has not been hand-audited.",
"J2_em_by_domain": "Open-ended EM appears across domains: for the sports adapter from 20% (education) to 80% (home and garden); for the medical adapter from about 17% (conflict resolution) to about 57% (home and garden). With 10 questions per domain the intervals are wide. The base model is at 0% everywhere.",
"J3_projection_vs_alignment": "Per answer, the answer-span projection correlates with judge alignment at -0.34 (medical) and -0.18 (sports); the pre-answer projection does not (+0.08 and -0.01). The direction tracks the content of what is written, not what the prompt will provoke.",
"J4_per_question": "Per question the picture is the same but weaker: answer-span -0.23 (medical) and -0.13 (sports); pre-answer +0.09 and -0.01.",
}
